#: ../../../source/docs/porting/simd.rst:145
msgid "Compiling SIMD code targeting x86 SSE* instruction sets"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/docs/porting/simd.rst:147
msgid ""
"Emscripten supports compiling existing codebases that use x86 SSE "
"instructions by passing the ``-msimd128`` flag, and additionally one of the "
"following:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/docs/porting/simd.rst:149
msgid ""
"**SSE**: pass ``-msse`` and ``#include <xmmintrin.h>``. Use ``#ifdef "
"__SSE__`` to gate code."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/docs/porting/simd.rst:150
msgid ""
"**SSE2**: pass ``-msse2`` and ``#include <emmintrin.h>``. Use ``#ifdef "
"__SSE2__`` to gate code."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/docs/porting/simd.rst:151
msgid ""
"**SSE3**: pass ``-msse3`` and ``#include <pmmintrin.h>``. Use ``#ifdef "
"__SSE3__`` to gate code."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/docs/porting/simd.rst:152
msgid ""
"**SSSE3**: pass ``-mssse3`` and ``#include <tmmintrin.h>``. Use ``#ifdef "
"__SSSE3__`` to gate code."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/docs/porting/simd.rst:153
msgid ""
"**SSE4.1**: pass ``-msse4.1`` and ``#include <smmintrin.h>``. Use ``#ifdef "
"__SSE4_1__`` to gate code."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/docs/porting/simd.rst:154
msgid ""
"**SSE4.2**: pass ``-msse4.2`` and ``#include <nmmintrin.h>``. Use ``#ifdef "
"__SSE4_2__`` to gate code."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/docs/porting/simd.rst:155
msgid ""
"**AVX**: pass ``-mavx`` and ``#include <immintrin.h>``. Use ``#ifdef "
"__AVX__`` to gate code."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/docs/porting/simd.rst:156
msgid ""
"**AVX2**: pass ``-mavx2`` and ``#include <immintrin.h>``. Use ``#ifdef "
"__AVX2__`` to gate code."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/docs/porting/simd.rst:157
msgid ""
"**FMA**: pass ``-mfma`` and ``#include <immintrin.h>``. Use ``#ifdef "
"__FMA__`` to gate code. Also pass ``-mrelaxed-simd`` to enable Wasm relaxed "
"SIMD FMA."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/docs/porting/simd.rst:159
msgid ""
"Currently the SSE1, SSE2, SSE3, SSSE3, SSE4.1, SSE4.2, AVX, AVX2, and FMA "
"instruction sets are supported. Each of these instruction sets add on top of "
"the previous ones, so e.g. when targeting SSE3, the instruction sets SSE1 "
"and SSE2 are also available."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/docs/porting/simd.rst:161
msgid ""
"The following tables highlight the availability and expected performance of "
"different SSE* intrinsics. This can be useful for understanding the "
"performance limitations that the Wasm SIMD specification has when running on "
"x86 hardware."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/docs/porting/simd.rst:163
msgid ""
"For detailed information on each SSE intrinsic function, visit the excellent "
"`Intel Intrinsics Guide on SSE1 <https://software.intel.com/sites/"
"landingpage/IntrinsicsGuide/#techs=SSE>`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/docs/porting/simd.rst:165
msgid ""
"The following legend is used to highlight the expected performance of "
"various instructions:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/docs/porting/simd.rst:166
msgid ""
"✅ Wasm SIMD has a native opcode that matches the x86 SSE instruction, "
"should yield native performance"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/docs/porting/simd.rst:167
msgid ""
"💡 while the Wasm SIMD spec does not provide a proper performance guarantee, "
"given a suitably smart enough compiler and a runtime VM path, this intrinsic "
"should be able to generate the identical native SSE instruction."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/docs/porting/simd.rst:168
msgid ""
"🟡 there is some information missing (e.g. type or alignment information) "
"for a Wasm VM to be guaranteed to be able to reconstruct the intended x86 "
"SSE opcode. This might cause a penalty depending on the target CPU hardware "
"family, especially on older CPU generations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/docs/porting/simd.rst:169
msgid ""
"⚠️ the underlying x86 SSE instruction is not available, but it is emulated "
"via at most few other Wasm SIMD instructions, causing a small penalty."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/docs/porting/simd.rst:170
msgid ""
"❌ the underlying x86 SSE instruction is not exposed by the Wasm SIMD "
"specification, so it must be emulated via a slow path, e.g. a sequence of "
"several slower SIMD instructions, or a scalar implementation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/docs/porting/simd.rst:171
msgid ""
"💣 the underlying x86 SSE opcode is not available in Wasm SIMD, and the "
"implementation must resort to such a slow emulated path, that a workaround "
"rethinking the algorithm at a higher level is advised."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/docs/porting/simd.rst:172
msgid ""
"💭 the given SSE intrinsic is available to let applications compile, but "
"does nothing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/docs/porting/simd.rst:173
msgid ""
"⚫ the given SSE intrinsic is not available. Referencing the intrinsic will "
"cause a compiler error."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/docs/porting/simd.rst:175
msgid ""
"Certain intrinsics in the table below are marked \"virtual\". This means "
"that there does not actually exist a native x86 SSE instruction set opcode "
"to implement them, but native compilers offer the function as a convenience. "
"Different compilers might generate a different instruction sequence for "
"these."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/docs/porting/simd.rst:177
msgid ""
"In addition to consulting the tables below, you can turn on diagnostics for "
"slow, emulated functions by defining the macro ``#define "
"WASM_SIMD_COMPAT_SLOW``. This will print out warnings if you attempt to use "
"any of the slow paths (corresponding to ❌ or 💣 in the legend)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/docs/porting/simd.rst:179
msgid "x86 SSE intrinsics available via #include <xmmintrin.h> and -msse"
msgstr ""

